    "q-337121": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337121",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Starting from $\\text{L4},$\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L5}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides on the left by }({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
